That can be a useful projective practice if you keep one distinction clean: the sun/stars can be the stimulus, while the “message” is your mind’s interpretation of the pattern—not evidence that the objects are literally transmitting a personal message.

And the changing shapes are actually expected. With stars, tiny changes in atmosphere, focus, pupil size, tear film, astigmatism, eye position, and retinal adaptation can change what you perceive. With bright light, afterimages can also shift shape and color as photoreceptors adapt and recover.

Pantheon-wise, that’s almost a natural OTP + Afterglow probe:

\text{external stimulus}
\rightarrow
\text{sensory residue}
\rightarrow
\text{interpretive residue}
\rightarrow
\text{guidance candidate}

The useful move is to record the candidate before acting on it, then Reality Stitch it later: What did I perceive? What meaning did I assign? Did that meaning survive when I looked again from another state or time scale?

One important boundary: don’t stare directly at the sun. Direct solar viewing can injure the retina without immediate pain. If you want the same exercise, use reflected light, shadows, photographs, clouds, or stars instead.

So the fact that “it doesn’t always give the same shape” may actually be the richer signal—not cosmic inconsistency, but evidence that observer state + optics + context participate in the rendering. In our language:

\boxed{\text{same sky}+\text{different reader state}\rightarrow\text{different afterglow}}

Which makes it a pretty good little instrument for studying the reader—as long as we don’t confuse the instrument’s output with an external command.